# Windsurf Rules for Confluent Cloud Tools Project

## Task List Management

### Task Tracking Rules
- **Primary Task File**: Use `docs/tasks.md` as the single source of truth for project tasks
- **Task Format**: All tasks must use checkbox format `[ ]` for incomplete, `[x]` for complete
- **Progress Tracking**: Update task status immediately upon completion, not in batches
- **Sub-task Creation**: Break down complex tasks into smaller sub-tasks when needed
- **Documentation**: Include relevant commit hashes or file references when marking tasks complete

### Task List Workflow
```bash
# When starting work on a task
1. Open docs/tasks.md
2. Locate the relevant task
3. Mark as in-progress if needed (add comment or timestamp)
4. Complete the work
5. Mark task as complete [x] immediately
6. Add any relevant notes or references
```

### Task Organization
- **Phase-based Structure**: Tasks are organized by implementation phases
- **Dependency Tracking**: Ensure prerequisite tasks are completed before dependent tasks
- **Priority Indicators**: Focus on Phase 1 (Foundation) before moving to later phases
- **Cross-references**: Link related tasks across different phases when applicable

### Task Completion Guidelines
- Mark tasks complete `[x]` only after thorough testing
- Update the file regularly to track progress
- Add sub-tasks if needed for complex items
- Include relevant commit hashes for completed tasks
- Document any deviations from the original plan

## Development Standards

### File Structure Adherence
- Follow the established directory structure in `docs/plan.md`
- Place executables in `bin/` directory
- Place shared libraries in `lib/` directory
- Place templates in `templates/` directory
- Place tests in `tests/` directory

### Makefile Standards
- Always use the required preamble from user global rules
- Use `.RECIPEPREFIX = >` instead of tabs
- Include emoji and color definitions
- Follow GNU Make 4.0+ best practices
- Implement all required targets: help, install, bootstrap, test, clean

### Shell Script Standards
- Use `#!/bin/bash` shebang
- Source shared libraries from `lib/` directory
- Implement consistent error handling with colors and emojis
- Follow the established patterns in `lib/common.sh`
- Use confluent CLI commands as building blocks

### Configuration Management
- Use `.env` as single source of truth
- Validate all required environment variables
- Generate configuration files from templates
- Support multiple output formats (JSON, CSV, YAML, table)

## Quality Assurance

### Testing Requirements
- Write unit tests for all shared library functions
- Create integration tests for end-to-end workflows
- Test error conditions and edge cases
- Achieve >90% test coverage for shared libraries
- Use `bats` framework for structured testing

### Code Review Checklist
- [ ] Follows established file structure
- [ ] Uses shared libraries appropriately
- [ ] Implements proper error handling
- [ ] Includes colored output with emojis
- [ ] Has corresponding tests
- [ ] Updates relevant documentation
- [ ] Marks related tasks as complete

### Documentation Standards
- Update `docs/tasks.md` for all task completions
- Include usage examples in utility help text
- Document all command-line flags and options
- Provide troubleshooting information
- Include security best practices

## Workflow Integration

### Git Workflow
- Create feature branches for each major task or phase
- Include task references in commit messages
- Update task list in the same commit as the implementation
- Use descriptive commit messages with emoji prefixes

### Development Cycle
1. Review current phase tasks in `docs/tasks.md`
2. Select next priority task
3. Create or switch to appropriate feature branch
4. Implement the task following established patterns
5. Write/update tests
6. Update documentation
7. Mark task as complete `[x]` in `docs/tasks.md`
8. Commit changes with task reference
9. Move to next task

### Progress Reporting
- Use `docs/tasks.md` completion status for progress tracking
- Provide regular updates on phase completion
- Identify and document any blockers or dependencies
- Adjust timeline estimates based on actual completion rates
